tool_metadata.description:
Use workflow_catalog for fixed multi-step tasks. Before writing or modifying the first .ets file in a task, use harmony_knowledge to read arkts-grammar-standards/recipes-core and consult the matching ArkTS or ArkUI references; review the Top-5 ArkTS traps before each additional .ets file. Keep project entry-page and routing declarations consistent with the selected module and product. After code changes, run applicable diagnostics first, then project_build, then an explicit verification step; only an explicit successful final assertion verifies UI outcomes. All HarmonyOS knowledge, Skills and guided workflow definitions are bundled in this MCP. Use them directly through tools; never install Skill files into the AI client. Large results are referenced artifacts. Use skill_workflow catalog/start for builtin plan/debug/spec/customize/arkts/repair/create/ui_test workflows. Each read/update returns current-phase Skill instructions, bundled knowledge, next tool actions and completion gates. Keep the run_id and revision, execute the indicated native MCP workflows, then attach applicable evidence. skill_manage catalog/read loads additional bundled references over MCP. For natural-language UI plans use ui_test: capture scopes, keep original requirements, execute bounded actions, check, read exact review images and complete ui_review before finish. On unchanged state inspect and replan; command acceptance alone never completes a test. Capture-only requests use ui_snapshot mode=image. Build workflows run a fresh full ArkTS preflight by default. Repair blocking diagnostics and recheck after changes; an explicit manual_override needs a recorded reason. Use workflow_run capacity/export/cleanup_plan/cleanup_apply for retained-state recovery instead of bypassing MCP deployment.

Use native SDK signing tools and developer cloud certificate/profile management. Mutations return a persistent run_id; use workflow_run for status, resume, cancellation and artifact reads. configure reads a private JSON descriptor from file, creates a new material directory at output, and selects options.name for the current product; existing configurations are preserved.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__deveco_tool__app_signature(args: {
  action: "inspect" | "configure" | "keypair" | "csr" | "sign" | "verify" | "certificates" | "certificate_create" | "certificate_delete" | "profile_create" | "profile_delete" | "devices" | "device_register";
  file?: string;
  // Build target per module, for example {entry: phone, shared: default}. These are Hvigor targets, independent of the HDC device target.
  module_targets?: { [key: string]: string; };
  options?: { [key: string]: string; };
  output?: string;
  product?: string;
  project_path?: string;
  request_key?: string;
  team_id?: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult<{ data?: unknown; error?: { code: string; details?: unknown; message: string; retryable: boolean; }; ok: boolean; request_id: string; }>>; };
```

